data.content:
The best approach is to use the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's a battle-tested library that wraps `File`, `Directory`, `Path`, and more behind an injectable `IFileSystem` interface. No need to write a custom wrapper.

### 1. Install the packages

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers  # in your test project
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
// In Program.cs or Startup.cs:
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class ReportGenerator(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        var outputDir = "/tmp/reports";

        if (!fileSystem.Directory.Exists(outputDir))
            fileSystem.Directory.CreateDirectory(outputDir);

        var reportPath = fileSystem.Path.Combine(outputDir, $"{reportName}.txt");
        fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(reportPath, "...");

        return reportPath;
    }
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers;

var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/tmp/reports/existing.txt", new MockFileData("old content") }
});

var generator = new ReportGenerator(mockFs);
generator.GenerateReport("sales");

// Assert the file was written
Assert.True(mockFs.File.Exists("/tmp/reports/sales.txt"));
```

`MockFileSystem` gives you a fully in-memory file system — no temp directories to clean up, no flaky tests from disk I/O. It covers `File.ReadAllText`, `File.WriteAllText`, `Directory.Exists`, and essentially the entire `System.IO` surface.
